/**
@page tutorials_exahype2_elastic_wave Elastodynamics in First Order Form

Assuming we have built the ExaHyPE2 engine and the core libraries sucessfully, we can build the Elastic Wave project:

    python3 elastic-wave.py

Assuming this was succesfull, you should see a number of files have appeared in your working directory.
We will ignore most of these for now, but know that these contain the implementation of the ExaHyPE2 project.
One file in particular is of interest to us now: the file simply named advection is the executable for our implementation.
We will therefore run this executable file by using the following command:

    ./elastic-wave-<dimensions>d

The solution folder contains the solution of our problem,
which we will take a look at as soon as we have an understanding of how our problem is defined.

In order to understand what it is that we have executed, let us take a look at the file which defined this project:

    elastic-wave.py

As this is a Python script, should surprise noone that we start with the following:

    import peano4
    import exahype2

These merely import the necessary packages from Peano4 and ExaHyPE2
which will let us define and build our project.
Speaking of defining our project, this happens in the next step:

    my_project = exahype2.Project( ["tutorials", "exahype2", "elasticwave"], "ElasticWaveTutorial", ".", executable="elastic-wave-" + str(dimensions) + "d" )

The parameters here are as follows: first the namespace that the project will have in C++,
then the name of the project itself, then where the project should be generated and finally the name of the executable.
Next we will define and add a solver to this project:

    my_solver = exahype2.solvers.fv.rusanov.GlobalAdaptiveTimeStep(
        name                  = "ElasticWaveSolver",
        patch_size            = 17,
        unknowns              = dimensions + 3,
        auxiliary_variables   = 0,
        min_volume_h          = 0.1 * 3.0**(-amr_levels),
        max_volume_h          = 0.1,
        time_step_relaxation  = 0.5
    )

Most of this should be self-explanatory and some pieces will be explained in more detail later,
but a few things deserve special mention here.
First is the solver itself, the type exahype2.solvers.fv.rusanov.GlobalAdaptiveTimeStep() indicates the type of solver.
fv stands for finite volumes, this describes the type of the solver, rusanov is the type of flux that is
used to solve the riemann problem at boundaries between cells. Finally global adaptive time step means the solver automatically
chooses an appropriate time step and uses this same time step over the entire domain.

For the parameters of this solver: "name=ElasticWaveSolver", this determines the name of the project and therefore of
the files generated by ExaHyPE2 that will define the application. "unknowns=dimensions + 3" and "auxiliary_variables=0" define the
variables of our equation, in this case we have a partial differential equation with 5 (or 6 in 3D) component variables but no auxiliary variables,
which are parameters that can be space-dependent but don't change for the duration of the simulation.

In this tutorial, we will implement the partial differential equation using a symbolic interface (a sympy installation is required).
We do not need to write any C++ code but completely rely on the code generator.

The elastic wave equation describes propagation of waves in elastic solids.
They are commonly derived from the conservation of momentum and the constitutive relation,
defined by Hookes's law. With the assumption that the relation between stress and strain can
be linearised for relatively small deformations this results in the linear elastic wave equation.
A material is described by it's density @f$`\rho`@f$, shear modulus @f$`\mu`@f$ and the Lame constant @f$`\lambda`@f$.

This tutorial implements the elastic wave equation in it's first order formulation:

@f$
    \partial_t \begin{pmatrix}
                u\\v\\ \sigma_{xx} \\ \sigma_{yy} \\ \sigma_{xy}
               \end{pmatrix}
               + \nabla \cdot F
               \begin{pmatrix}
                u\\v\\ \sigma_{xx} \\ \sigma_{yy} \\ \sigma_{xy}
               \end{pmatrix} = 0,
@f$

With @f$`F=\left(F_1,F_2\right)`@f$ being a tensor describing the linear flux:

@f$
    F_1 =
        \begin{pmatrix}
        0 & 0 & 1/\rho &  0 & 0 \\
        0 & 0 &      0 &  0 & 1/\rho \\
        \left(\lambda + 2\mu \right) & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0  \\
        \lambda&0&0&0&0 \\
        0&\mu&0&0&0
        \end{pmatrix}
@f$

and

@f$
    F_2 =
        \begin{pmatrix}
        0 & 0 & 0 &  0 & 1/\rho \\
        0 & 0 &   0 &  1/\rho &  0\\
        0 & \lambda & 0 & 0 & 0  \\
        0 & \left(\lambda + 2\mu \right) & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
        \mu & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0
        \end{pmatrix}
@f$

In elastic materials, there are two types of waves which can propagate through the domain
p-waves and s-waves, their speeds are

@f$
    c_s=\sqrt{\frac{\mu}{\rho}}
@f$

and

@f$
    c_p=\sqrt{\frac{\lambda + 2 \mu}{\rho}}
@f$

These speeds also define the eigenvalues of the flux:

@f$
    \left( -c_p, -c_s , 0 , c_s , c_p \right)
@f$

    pde = exahype2.symhype.FirstOrderConservativePDEFormulation(unknowns=dimensions + 3, auxiliary_variables=0, dimensions=dimensions)
    v = pde.name_Q_entries(0, dimensions, "v")
    sigma = pde.name_Q_entries(dimensions, 3, "sigma")

    pde.initial_values[0] = 0
    pde.initial_values[1] = 0
    pde.initial_values[2] = 0
    pde.initial_values[3] = 0
    pde.initial_values[4] = 0

    pde.boundary_values[0] = 0
    pde.boundary_values[1] = 0
    pde.boundary_values[2] = 0
    pde.boundary_values[3] = 0
    pde.boundary_values[4] = 0

    import sympy
    rho = sympy.symbols("rho")
    cp = sympy.symbols("cp")
    cs = sympy.symbols("cs")

    # Lamé parameters
    mu = rho * cs * cs
    lamb = rho * cp * cp - 2.0 * mu

    # Flux [unknowns, dimensions]
    pde.F[0, 0] = -1.0/rho*sigma[0]
    pde.F[1, 0] = -1.0/rho*sigma[2]
    pde.F[2, 0] = -(2.0*mu*lamb) * v[0]
    pde.F[3, 0] = -lamb * v[0]
    pde.F[4, 0] = -mu * v[1]

    pde.F[0, 1] = -1.0/rho*sigma[2]
    pde.F[1, 1] = -1.0/rho*sigma[1]
    pde.F[2, 1] = -lamb * v[1]
    pde.F[3, 1] = -(2.0*mu*lamb) * v[1]
    pde.F[4, 1] = -mu * v[0]

    pde.eigenvalues[0] = cp
    pde.eigenvalues[1] = cs
    pde.eigenvalues[2] = 0
    pde.eigenvalues[3] = -cs
    pde.eigenvalues[4] = -cp

    t0 = sympy.symbols("t0")
    M0 = sympy.symbols("M0")
    t = sympy.symbols("t")
    force = M0 * t / (t0 * t0) * (sympy.exp(-t/t0))

    pde.sources[0] = 0
    pde.sources[1] = 0
    pde.sources[2] = 0
    pde.sources[3] = 0
    pde.sources[4] = force

    pde.substitute_expression(rho, 2.7)
    pde.substitute_expression(cp, 6.0)
    pde.substitute_expression(cs, 3.464)
    pde.substitute_expression(t0, 0.7)
    pde.substitute_expression(M0, 1000.0)

We use the Python pde class to get the generated C++ implementation and set it in our solver accordingly:

    my_solver.set_implementation(
        initial_conditions    = pde.implementation_of_initial_conditions(),
        boundary_conditions   = pde.implementation_of_boundary_conditions(),
        flux                  = pde.implementation_of_flux(),
        eigenvalues           = pde.implementation_of_max_eigenvalue(),

        source_term           = """
            const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double> sourceLocation({10, 10});
            if (tarch::la::norm2(volumeCentre - tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>(sourceLocation)) < 0.1) {
                %s
            }
        """ % pde.implementation_of_sources(),

        refinement_criterion  = """
            constexpr bool useDynamicAMR = false;
            const tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double> sourceLocation({10, 10});
            const double distance = tarch::la::norm2(volumeCentre - tarch::la::Vector<Dimensions, double>(sourceLocation));
            auto command = tarch::la::equals(t, 0.0)
                ? (0.8 < distance && distance < 1.0) ? ::exahype2::RefinementCommand::Refine : ::exahype2::RefinementCommand::Keep
                : ::exahype2::RefinementCommand::Keep;

            if constexpr (useDynamicAMR) {
                if (tarch::la::greater(t, 0.0)) {
                    if (Q[4] >  0.4) {
                        command = ::exahype2::RefinementCommand::Refine;
                    }
                    if (Q[4] < 0.2) {
                        command = ::exahype2::RefinementCommand::Erase;
                    }
                }
            }
            return command;
        """
    )

Next we define the parameters for our simulation, this is where we define the volume of our domain and the number of dimensions
for example, but also where basic information about the plotting behaviour is defined:

    if dimensions == 2:
        size    = [20.0, 20.0]
        offset  = [-5.0, 0.0]
    else:
        size    = [20.0, 20.0, 20.0]
        offset  = [-5.0, 0.0, 0.0]

    my_project.set_global_simulation_parameters(
        dimensions            = dimensions,
        size                  = size,
        offset                = offset,
        min_end_time          = 0.20,
        max_end_time          = 0.20,
        first_plot_time_stamp = 0.0,
        time_in_between_plots = 0.001, # snapshot interval
        periodic_BC           = [False, False, False]
    )

Finally, given all the information above we can generate and build a working application.

    my_project.set_load_balancing( "toolbox::loadbalancing::RecursiveBipartition", "new ::exahype2::LoadBalancingConfiguration()" )
    my_project.set_Peano4_installation( "../../../", mode=peano4.output.CompileMode.Release )
    my_project = my_project.generate_Peano4_project(verbose=True)
    my_project.build(make_clean_first=True, throw_away_data_after_build=True)

*/
